>
  <p class="text-lg">
    A solar-powered, wireless sensor network for automated farm irrigation. Remote sensor nodes 
    monitor soil moisture and environmental conditions, transmitting data over LoRa radio to a 
    central hub for monitoring and watering control.
  </p>

  <h2>System Architecture</h2>
  <p>
    The system uses a hub-and-spoke topology. Multiple remote sensor nodes communicate with 
    a centralized Raspberry Pi hub over long-range LoRa radio (433 MHz, SX1278 modules), 
    providing coverage across large agricultural areas without Wi-Fi infrastructure.
  </p>

  <h2>Sensor Nodes</h2>
  <p>
    Each node is built around an ESP32 microcontroller paired with capacitive soil moisture 
    sensors and DHT22 temperature/humidity sensors. Nodes are solar-powered using small panels 
    with TP4056 charge controllers and LiPo batteries for off-grid, maintenance-free operation.
  </p>

  <h2>Central Hub</h2>
  <p>
    The Raspberry Pi hub receives sensor data, stores it in a time-series database, and serves 
    a Grafana web dashboard for real-time monitoring and historical analysis. Remote access is 
    enabled through Tailscale, allowing management from anywhere.
  </p>

  <h2>Key Features</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Off-grid operation</strong> — solar powered with battery backup on each node</li>
    <li><strong>Long-range wireless</strong> — LoRa radio for km-range communication without Wi-Fi</li>
    <li><strong>Web dashboard</strong> — Grafana visualization with remote access via Tailscale</li>
    <li><strong>Automated control</strong> — threshold-based watering triggers from sensor readings</li>
  </ul>
</ProjectLayout>
